Hi Mark,

I am writing to formally update you that I have paused the insulation work.=
 The mold issue has now appeared in a second room, and the root cause has b=
een confirmed as a severe ventilation imbalance (Vacuum Effect) caused by t=
he roof system installed in April 2024.

I have consulted with independent roofing specialists to verify the physics=
. I am submitting this Warranty Claim based on manufacturer specifications,=
 your original contract (Quote #203), and your recent text admission regard=
ing the airflow.

1. The Engineering Failure (The Math)

Your Quote #203 guaranteed "Adequate Venting." The current system is mathem=
atically incapable of providing it:

  *   Exhaust: The 60-foot Omni Ridge Vent pulls 1,080 sq. in. of air.

  *   Intake: The 12 SV-50 vents provide (at best) 600 sq. in. of intake.

  *   The Result: A massive intake deficit that creates negative pressure, =
sucking warm, dirty air from my living space into the attic.

2. Your Admission of Incompatibility

In your text to me on Jan 11, you stated: "If these soffits were to get con=
verted / vented. Those low air vents would alter the air flow by exhaust in=
stead of drawing air."

You are correct. This confirms that the SV-50 vents you installed cannot co=
exist with a proper intake system. To fix the house, we must install contin=
uous soffit venting (to get the required 1,080 sq. in.), which means your S=
V-50 vents must be removed to prevent them from short-circuiting the airflo=
w.

3. Product Misuse (Manufacturer Spec)

Furthermore, I have verified with Menzies Metal Products that the SV-50 is =
"Not to be used as an intake vent." It is designed for exhaust on steep roo=
fs. Using them as intake on a 4/12 slope is a prohibited application that v=
oids the performance specs.

________________________________
The Resolution Plan (The "Split Solution")

I want to resolve this amicably. I understand that cutting stucco soffits i=
s specialized work, so I am offering a solution that saves you from that la=
bor/expense.

My Part (The Upgrade): I will hire and pay for a specialized siding company=
 (e.g., Parker Johnston) to cut the continuous soffit vents. This ensures t=
he house finally gets the 1,080 sq. in. of intake it needs.

Your Part (Warranty Repair): You simply need to remove the 12 non-compliant=
 SV-50 vents and patch the roof deck/shingles at your cost.

Requirement for the Repair: To ensure the integrity of the roof deck, the p=
atch must be done correctly:

  1.  Plywood plugs securely fastened.

  2.  New underlayment (Roofnado) tucked shingle-fashion under the existing=
 upper course (not just pasted on top).

  3.  Photo Verification: For my insurance records, I require a photo of th=
e underlayment install at each of the 12 locations before the shingles are =
nailed down.

Next Steps: This solution relieves you of the difficult stucco work while c=
orrecting the "Adequate Venting" failure. Please confirm you agree to this =
plan so I can coordinate your patch crew with my siding crew.

Regards,

